; specs/policy/budget_discipline.t27 -- the agent-facing discipline for the
; foreign budget, so adapting to the caps is reading one spec and running one
; command, not paying the gate's refusals for hours (owner, 2026-10-27).
; The LAW is specs/policy/own_language.t27: this card adds no rule of its own
; -- its tests replay the cases an agent meets against the real gate function,
; so the refusal codes and the boundaries are pinned where the next session
; will look first.
; The two caps are INDEPENDENT, and a diff can pass either and fail the other:
;   1. at most 40 hand-written foreign lines ADDED in one file (80 per PR);
;   2. added <= deleted across the whole diff (shrink-only).
; No exception and no label lifts either. Measured before you commit:
;   git diff --cached --numstat --no-renames   (added TAB deleted TAB path)
; Foreign = everything not .t27 / .md / .txt / .json / a generated root.
; The honest way under both when a fix is bigger than the cap: compress the
; additions you themselves made (docs to fewer lines, branches folded to one
; line, concat pieces merged with identical emitted text), delete blank doc
; separators in the same files, or split the change -- a count PR first, the
; verdict PR second. Never a second registry, never --no-verify.

module PolicyBudgetDiscipline;

use policy::own_language;

; The gate's answer is the NUMBER of refusals, not a code: one per file over
; the 40 per-file cap, one when the counted files together pass 80 added, one
; when they gain more than they lose. 0 lets the commit through; a diff can
; collect several refusals at once (96 added in one file collects three).

; The replayed cases, in order: 96 added is over the 80 PR cap even though net
; -8 (#12711: net-negative does not save an over-cap diff); 74 in one file is
; over the 40 per-file cap though net -1 (the shrink rule does not lift the
; cap); 40 is exactly at the per-file cap and passes; 40/43 (net -3) is the
; shape that landed as #12777; 19/8 shows small numbers change nothing; 74/75
; is net -1 and still refused -- the cap counts added lines alone.
test the_session_refusals_replay_exactly {
    var out : [512]u8 = undefined;
    assert check_budget("96\t88\tbootstrap/src/test_report.rs\n", 35, &out, 512) == 3;
    assert check_budget("74\t74\tbootstrap/src/test_report.rs\n", 35, &out, 512) == 1;
    assert check_budget("40\t40\ta.rs\n", 11, &out, 512) == 0;
    assert check_budget("40\t43\ta.rs\n", 11, &out, 512) == 0;
    assert check_budget("19\t8\ta.rs\n", 10, &out, 512) == 1;
    assert check_budget("74\t75\ta.rs\n", 11, &out, 512) == 1;
}

; 40 added passes the per-file cap (41 would not); the shrink boundary is
; inclusive: added == deleted passes.
test the_boundaries_are_inclusive {
    var out : [512]u8 = undefined;
    assert check_budget("40\t40\ta.rs\n", 11, &out, 512) == 0;
    assert check_budget("3\t3\ta.rs\n", 9, &out, 512) == 0;
}

; The REPEATED TRAPS of 2026-10-27, each paid for more than twice:
; 1. THE GATE-VIEW RULE: every gate reads `origin/master...HEAD` (three dots,
;    merge-base to HEAD). A commit-view numstat lies about what the gate will
;    see; measure the gate view BEFORE committing, not after the refusal.
; 2. THE SEMICOLON TRAP: a `;` comment lives only at module level in a .t27
;    spec. Inside a test or fn body it is a parse error ("Unexpected token in
;    expression: Semicolon"), and so is a lone `;` separator line. Caught four
;    times in one day.
; 3. THE MERGE-COMMIT VIEW: while a merge commit is being built locally, the
;    pre-commit gate reads the WHOLE incoming master diff as added foreign
;    lines, so bringing master INTO a branch can never pass it ("no exception
;    and no label lifts this limit"). The rebase replays one commit at a time
;    and each replayed diff is the branch's own lines -- rebase, not merge, is
;    the mechanical DIRTY fix.
; 4. THE SEAL CONFLICT RULE: when a rebase conflicts in .trinity/seals/*.json
;    and both sides carry the SAME spec_hash, the seals describe identical
;    content and the newer sealed_at wins (take master's side; --ours during
;    a rebase). The seal gates re-verify the tree either way.
; The BOUNDARY of the honest cut (2026-10-27, #11806): compressing YOUR OWN
; additions is the technique -- compressing OTHERS' memory-comments (debug
; histories, contracts) to pass the gate is gaming, and a net-positive diff
; on someone else's branch cannot be fixed from inside at all. The move is:
; land your verified work on a local branch, save the patch, and ask for the
; owner's verdict (label, or the author's own rework).
; The same incident taught branch recovery: deleting a PR's head branch
; auto-closes the PR, and the restore is the local repo's FULL 40-char SHA
; pushed with a BRANCH refspec (`git push origin <branch>:refs/heads/<name>`
; after `git branch -f restore <sha>`) -- never `SHA:refs/...` in zsh, where
; the two tokens concatenate into a broken refspec -- then `gh pr reopen`.
; Script against `headRefOid`, never `headRefName`: the NAME can belong to a
; different branch than you assume.
; The verdict words this discipline feeds forward (specs/hosting/row.t27
; T27B_WORDS, appended never inserted -- stored codes must not move):
; pass_vacuous checked nothing at run time; pass_ct verified at compile time;
; CHECKED executed runtime asserts. The law of the split is
; specs/compiler/theory/toolchain.t27 report_state.
